From: pgf@srl02.cacs.usl.edu (Phil G. Fraering)
Date: Sun, 18 Sep 1994 17:26:42 -0500
Subject: [#94-9-250] Omega Point

Here's an interesting idea that's lodged itself somewhere
in my hindbrain. 

Let's postulate that Tipler is right. The Omega Point is reached.
A sentient species becomes their own God... but it's the species
over in some K-2 star system several thousand light-years away,
several million years in the future. This Omega Point never really
even finds out about humanity, because when the evidence of humanity
was destroyed after some great catastrophe we weren't able to
stop because the Entropians won and civilization was replaced by
some sort of medieval communism.

So humanity isn't de-facto ressurected, just those two-headed guys
over in Hydra.

Personally, I'm not sure there's anything immediate I can do to
instantiate the Singularity, but I don't really feel it's safe to
lie back and think the Omega Point will handle it all. As K.
Eric Drexler would phrase it, my mind exists in the period after
the Copernican revolution. I suspect Tipler's mind might exist in
the period before the Copernican revolution.

It seems to me that the existance of God would discredit rather than
strengthen the idea that man is the center of the universe.

Phil

